      "body": "## Purpose\n\nWith a hybrid model (Mamba-style / linear-attention layers), prefix caching (`mamba_cache_mode=\"align\"`), FULL CUDA graphs and data parallelism, a DP dummy run can write linear-attention state into KV-cache blocks owned by other live requests. Those requests then read corrupted K/V and produce NaN logits.\n\n**When it happens.** An engine runs dummy batches (`execute_dummy_batch`) while its persistent batch still holds live requests. The usual trigger is `pause_generation(mode=\"keep\")` with DP: until all DP ranks agree to pause, the paused ranks keep stepping dummy batches. RL frameworks use this for in-flight weight updates. In our runs that meant 2–56 dummy steps per pause, with every running request still in the batch.\n\n**Root cause.** In `GPUModelRunner._dummy_run` (FULL mode):\n\n- It commits the live block table to the GPU with `commit_block_table(num_reqs_padded)`, then builds attention metadata from it.\n- It calls `_build_attention_metadata(num_reqs=num_reqs_padded, ...)` without a separate `num_reqs_padded`, so the \"fill unused rows with `NULL_BLOCK_ID`\" range in `_get_block_table` is empty. Every dummy row keeps a live request's block IDs.\n- Dummy `seq_lens` are 1. In align mode `mamba_get_block_table_tensor` picks block index `(seq_len - 1) // block_size = 0` of each row's mamba block list. For a long request, entry 0 is an old state block that the allocator has since freed and reused, often as another request's attention block.\n- Attention KV writes are disabled for dummy runs (`slot_mapping = -1`), but the mamba/GDN kernels have no such guard. They update the state in place at that block.\n- Attention and mamba layers share the same physical KV-cache tensors in the hybrid allocator, so the state write overwrites the other request's K/V. The fp32 recurrent state read as bf16 contains NaN patterns, and the next real step reads it.\n\nPIECEWISE/eager dummy runs build no attention metadata, so the cache layers skip them and the bug does not appear there. The newer model runner (`vllm/v1/worker/gpu/`) already builds separate block tables for dummy runs (`get_dummy_block_tables`).\n\n**Fix.** In `_dummy_run`, point every row of the dummy batch at `NULL_BLOCK_ID` in all KV-cache groups, instead of committing the live rows. Dummy kernels then only touch the reserved null block, as padding rows already do in real steps. Real steps are unaffected: `_prepare_inputs` re-commits the block table from its CPU copy at the start of every step.\n\n## Test Plan\n\n- Hybrid MoE model with 11 full-attention and 31 KDA (`GDN_ATTN` backend) layers, 1 node with DP=4 and EP=4, FULL_AND_PIECEWISE CUDA graphs, prefix caching on. Long generations (`ignore_eos`, 4K tokens) were paused with `pause_generation(mode=\"keep\")` and the weights updated in place while 32 requests per engine were in flight.\n- An instrumentation probe checksummed every KV-cache block across each pause and recorded the state blocks each GDN metadata build handed to the kernels.\n\n## Test Result\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| requests with NaN logits after an in-flight pause | 90 over 16 pauses | 0 over 19 pauses (2 runs) |\n| pauses during which dummy steps changed live requests' blocks | 30 / 64 checks | 0 / 76 checks |\n| decode throughput per step | — | unchanged |\n\n- Before the fix, the probe showed the dummy row's stale entry-0 state blocks had become other requests' attention blocks. Exactly those requests had non-finite K/V before the next real step, starting right after the bf16 conv-state bytes, and they produced NaN logits on that step.\n- PIECEWISE graphs (dummy runs without metadata) gave 0 NaNs and no pause writes on the same workload.\n- A 16-node RL production run with the fix had 0 NaN sequences over 17 in-flight weight updates. The same configuration without the fix masked 150 NaN sequences in its first 19 steps.\n\n\n---\n<a href=\"https://app.blacksmith.sh/vllm-project/codesmith/vllm/pr/63776?autoLogin=true&ref=codesmith_pr_footer\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-dark-v3.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-light-v3.svg\"><img alt=\"View with [code]smith\" src=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-dark-v3-3x.png\" width=\"162\" height=\"28\"></picture></a> <a href=\"https://backend.blacksmith.sh/track/enable-autofix?expires=1795618963&installation_model_id=425286&pr_number=63776&ref=codesmith_pr_footer&repository=vllm-project%2Fvllm&return_to=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fvllm-project%2Fvllm%2Fpull%2F63776&signature=69fa3c51d23811f74e24f90d348ab1a6802f3dd63ed32c8bbbd75e6c14e5ab47\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/autofix-with-codesmith-dark.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/autofix-with-codesmith-light.svg\"><img alt=\"Autofix with [code]smith\" src=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/autofix-with-codesmith-dark-3x.png\" width=\"190\" height=\"28\"></picture></a>\n<sup>Need help on this PR? Tag <code>@codesmith-bot</code> with what you need.</sup>\n\n<!-- codesmith:footer -->\n---\n<a href=\"https://app.blacksmith.sh/vllm-project/codesmith/vllm/pr/63776?autoLogin=true&ref=codesmith_pr_footer\"><picture><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: dark)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-dark-v3.svg\"><source media=\"(prefers-color-scheme: light)\" srcset=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-light-v3.svg\"><img alt=\"View with [code]smith\" src=\"https://pr-comments-assets.blacksmith.sh/codesmith/view-with-codesmith-dark-v3-3x.png\" width=\"162\" height=\"28\"></picture></a> 